{
  "version": 1,
  "_comment": "European ACARS reception — freqhops through the four ICAO-EUR channels: 131.725 MHz (AOA primary + CPDLC), 131.525 / 131.475 / 131.825 MHz (SITA + backups).  Modulation is 2400-baud MSK (mark 2400 Hz / space 1200 Hz) AM-carried on a 25 kHz airband channel.  Bursts are short (100-500 ms) and sporadic, so a busy channel may take a minute of dwell to catch anything; the primary 131.725 gets the longest slot.  Hardware: HackRF at 2 MSPS (its minimum sample rate); RTL-SDR users can drop bw_hz to 250 kSPS.  Antenna must cover ~131 MHz — a broadband donut/loop, a 1/4-wave ground plane cut for ~131 MHz (57 cm radiator + four 63 cm radials at 45° down), or a discone all work.  Do NOT use the SAWbird+NOAA filter here: its passband is 135-139 MHz, which puts 131 MHz 40 dB into the stopband and kills every ACARS transmission before it reaches the SDR.  A wideband LNA (Nooelec LaNA, RTL-SDR Blog LNA, etc.) helps in fringe areas — external gain reduces the load on HackRF's internal amps.  gain_db 20 is a starting point for HackRF with an external LNA in the chain; dial down if the constellation view is a saturated blob (compression), up if bursts barely rise above the waterfall noise floor.",
  "center_hz": 131725000.0,
